From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 16:32:09 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] feat(control): links on a ghostty pane - hover, tooltip,
 ctrl+click (KD-20)

Reported from use: holding ctrl highlighted URLs, but clicking one opened
nothing. Both halves are the same gap - libghostty was never told where
the pointer was.

SetHoveredCell and ClearHoveredCell were empty, so the only mouse
positions the engine ever received came from a selection or from VT mouse
reporting, and its cursor sat wherever the last click had left it. ghostty
re-runs link detection whenever the modifiers change, against that cached
position, so ctrl lit up the link at a stale point: real highlighting, of
the wrong cell. GetHyperlink returned empty unconditionally, so WT's
ctrl+click branch never ran, and ghostty's own opener could not run either
- the selection path sends no press at all for a single click, and
GHOSTTY_ACTION_OPEN_URL was unhandled.

The Phase 6 gate deferred this (GD-01) believing it needed a new
libghostty entry point. It did not. GHOSTTY_ACTION_MOUSE_OVER_LINK already
reports the URL under the pointer; the C API had been searched for a
getter and the answer was an action. No ghostty patch changes.

SetHoveredCell now sends one position per cell the pointer enters,
carrying the modifiers the last key event carried - the same events
ghostty derives its own modifier state from, so the two cannot disagree.
The selection path marks the pointer events it has already driven, so the
hover stands aside mid-drag rather than re-sending the position with the
keyboard's modifiers and flipping a block selection to linear. The first
hover after a drag is also where the synthesised release now lands, since
WT reports no release outside VT mouse mode; without it these positions
would read as a drag that never ended.

MOUSE_OVER_LINK fills GetHyperlink, HoveredUriText and HoveredCell and
raises HoveredHyperlinkChanged, so WT's existing ctrl+click and tooltip
work unchanged. OPEN_URL is claimed and raised as OpenHyperlink, so a link
ghostty decides to open goes through WT's opener and its refused-scheme
dialog instead of libghostty's rundll32 fallback. A release WT synthesised
is flagged while it is sent, because ghostty opens a link on release and
that release is inferred rather than reported - otherwise a double-click
inside a URL followed by ctrl and a mouse move would open it.

Measured in a pane, a portable build of this tree: ctrl+hover over
ftp://example.com/kd20 underlines the URL under the pointer, where before
nothing under the pointer was ever considered, and a ctrl+click on a typed
http://www.google.com opens the browser. unitControl 67/68, the one skip
pre-existing.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp    | 190 ++++++++++++++++--
 .../TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.h      |  21 ++
 2 files changed, 198 insertions(+), 13 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
index 6c0a9f803..5a723a2f0 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
@@ -568,6 +568,49 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
             return true;
         }
 
+        // The link under the pointer - the only way an embedder learns there
+        // is one, because ghostty owns link detection (its `link-url` regex and
+        // OSC 8) and draws the highlight itself. An empty URL means the pointer
+        // left the link. Reported only when the highlight changes, so this is
+        // not a per-move event.
+        case GHOSTTY_ACTION_MOUSE_OVER_LINK:
+        {
+            // The bytes belong to an arena that dies with this call, so the
+            // copy happens here rather than on the UI thread.
+            const auto& link = action.action.mouse_over_link;
+            const winrt::hstring uri{ link.len > 0 ? til::u8u16(std::string_view{ link.url, link.len }) : std::wstring{} };
+            _raiseOnUiThread([this, uri]() {
+                if (_hoveredUri == uri)
+                {
+                    return;
+                }
+                _hoveredUri = uri;
+                HoveredHyperlinkChanged.raise(*this, nullptr);
+            });
+            return true;
+        }
+
+        // ghostty decided a link should open: a ctrl+click it handled itself,
+        // or an `open_url` binding. Windows Terminal has its own opener, with
+        // the confirmation dialog for schemes it does not trust, so this is
+        // handed up rather than opened here - and *claimed*, because a
+        // libghostty told that nobody handled it falls back to its own opener
+        // (rundll32 url.dll,FileProtocolHandler, os/open.zig), which would step
+        // around that dialog.
+        case GHOSTTY_ACTION_OPEN_URL:
+        {
+            if (_suppressOpenUrl)
+            {
+                return true;
+            }
+            const auto& open = action.action.open_url;
+            const winrt::hstring uri{ til::u8u16(std::string_view{ open.url, open.len }) };
+            _raiseOnUiThread([this, uri]() {
+                OpenHyperlink.raise(*this, winrt::make<implementation::OpenHyperlinkEventArgs>(uri));
+            });
+            return true;
+        }
+
         case GHOSTTY_ACTION_RENDERER_HEALTH:
         {
             if (action.action.renderer_health != GHOSTTY_RENDERER_HEALTH_HEALTHY)
@@ -809,6 +852,19 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
 
 #pragma region Input
 
+    // The modifier bits libghostty wants, from the modifier state Windows
+    // Terminal reports on the event that carried them. Every path into
+    // libghostty that takes modifiers comes through here, so they cannot drift
+    // apart.
+    static ghostty_input_mods_e GhosttyMods(const ControlKeyStates states) noexcept
+    {
+        auto mods = GHOSTTY_MODS_NONE;
+        if (states.IsShiftPressed()) { mods = static_cast<ghostty_input_mods_e>(mods | GHOSTTY_MODS_SHIFT); }
+        if (states.IsCtrlPressed()) { mods = static_cast<ghostty_input_mods_e>(mods | GHOSTTY_MODS_CTRL); }
+        if (states.IsAltPressed()) { mods = static_cast<ghostty_input_mods_e>(mods | GHOSTTY_MODS_ALT); }
+        return mods;
+    }
+
     // The text a key press produces, as UTF-8, or empty for keys that produce
     // none. ToUnicode rather than waiting for the CharacterReceived event, so
     // the text arrives *with* the key ghostty is given rather than as a
@@ -835,11 +891,14 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         ev.keycode = GhosttyNativeKeycode(scanCode, (modifiers.Value() & ENHANCED_KEY) != 0);
         ev.composing = false;
 
-        auto mods = GHOSTTY_MODS_NONE;
-        if (modifiers.IsShiftPressed()) { mods = static_cast<ghostty_input_mods_e>(mods | GHOSTTY_MODS_SHIFT); }
-        if (modifiers.IsCtrlPressed()) { mods = static_cast<ghostty_input_mods_e>(mods | GHOSTTY_MODS_CTRL); }
-        if (modifiers.IsAltPressed()) { mods = static_cast<ghostty_input_mods_e>(mods | GHOSTTY_MODS_ALT); }
+        const auto mods = GhosttyMods(modifiers);
         ev.mods = mods;
+
+        // Kept for the pointer, which has to state the modifiers on every
+        // position it sends and is handed none by WT's hover call. Not a second
+        // source of truth: ghostty derives its own modifier state from these
+        // same key events, so the two can only ever agree.
+        _keyMods = mods;
         // consumed_mods is set below, once the text is known - it cannot be
         // decided before we have seen what the layout produced.
 
@@ -1008,6 +1067,11 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
     {
         _trace(L"LostFocus (surface=%p)", _surface);
         _focused = false;
+
+        // What is held while another window owns the keyboard is not ours to
+        // report. Keeping it would let a ctrl released elsewhere go on
+        // highlighting links here on the next hover.
+        _keyMods = GHOSTTY_MODS_NONE;
         if (_surface)
         {
             ghostty_surface_set_focus(_surface, false);
@@ -1044,10 +1108,7 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
             return false;
         }
 
-        auto mods = GHOSTTY_MODS_NONE;
-        if (states.IsShiftPressed()) { mods = static_cast<ghostty_input_mods_e>(mods | GHOSTTY_MODS_SHIFT); }
-        if (states.IsCtrlPressed()) { mods = static_cast<ghostty_input_mods_e>(mods | GHOSTTY_MODS_CTRL); }
-        if (states.IsAltPressed()) { mods = static_cast<ghostty_input_mods_e>(mods | GHOSTTY_MODS_ALT); }
+        const auto mods = GhosttyMods(states);
 
         // Every report carries a position, so the pointer moves first whatever
         // the event is. ghostty derives the reported cell from this.
@@ -1254,7 +1315,15 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         {
             return;
         }
+
+        // ghostty opens a link on the *release* of a click over one
+        // (Surface.zig, processLinks). This release is not a click - WT never
+        // said the button came up, we are inferring it - so a URL that opened
+        // out of it would be one nobody asked for. The action arrives
+        // synchronously, on this thread, so a flag separates the two.
+        _suppressOpenUrl = true;
         ghostty_surface_mouse_button(_surface, GHOSTTY_MOUSE_RELEASE, GHOSTTY_MOUSE_LEFT, GHOSTTY_MODS_NONE);
+        _suppressOpenUrl = false;
         _mouseDown = false;
     }
 
@@ -1282,6 +1351,7 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
 
         _releaseMouseIfHeld();
         _bindingAction("clear_selection");
+        _selectionDroveTheMouse();
         _mouseTo(positionCore, _selectionMods());
 
         // The press carries no modifiers, and that is deliberate: ghostty reads
@@ -1309,6 +1379,7 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
     void GhosttyControlCore::SetEndSelectionPoint(const Core::Point positionCore)
     {
         // Only meaningful mid-drag, which is the only time WT calls it.
+        _selectionDroveTheMouse();
         _mouseTo(positionCore, _selectionMods());
     }
 
@@ -1350,6 +1421,7 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         // click extends the previous selection instead of starting one.
         _releaseMouseIfHeld();
         _bindingAction("clear_selection");
+        _selectionDroveTheMouse();
 
         if (numberOfClicks > 1)
         {
@@ -1705,11 +1777,103 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
     bool GhosttyControlCore::ShouldShowSelectCommand() { return false; }
     bool GhosttyControlCore::ShouldShowSelectOutput() { return false; }
 
-    void GhosttyControlCore::SetHoveredCell(Core::Point /*terminalPosition*/) {}
-    void GhosttyControlCore::ClearHoveredCell() {}
-    winrt::hstring GhosttyControlCore::GetHyperlink(const Core::Point /*position*/) const { return {}; }
-    winrt::hstring GhosttyControlCore::HoveredUriText() const { return {}; }
-    IReference<Core::Point> GhosttyControlCore::HoveredCell() const { return nullptr; }
+    // Where the pointer is. Nothing here looks for a URL: ghostty finds links
+    // itself and reports the one under its *own* cursor as MOUSE_OVER_LINK, so
+    // all this owes it is a cursor that is where the pointer is.
+    //
+    // Until this existed the only positions ghostty ever got came from a
+    // selection or from VT mouse reporting, so its cursor sat wherever the last
+    // click had left it. Holding ctrl still lit up a link - ghostty re-runs
+    // link detection when the modifiers change - but it was the link at that
+    // stale point, and ctrl+click opened nothing at all (KD-20).
+    void GhosttyControlCore::SetHoveredCell(Core::Point terminalPosition)
+    {
+        // A drag owns the mouse. ControlInteractivity calls this at the end of
+        // every pointer move, a drag's moves included, and the drag has just
+        // sent this same position with the *selection's* modifiers - repeating
+        // it with the keyboard's would turn a block selection back into a
+        // linear one halfway through.
+        //
+        // The flag is per event, so it also swallows the first hover after an
+        // auto-scroll tick, which drives the mouse with no pointer move behind
+        // it. That costs one mouse move of latency and nothing else.
+        if (_selectionDrove)
+        {
+            _selectionDrove = false;
+            return;
+        }
+
+        if (!_surface || _closing)
+        {
+            return;
+        }
+
+        // The application owns the mouse. It is already being told where the
+        // pointer is, by SendMouseEvent, with the real event's modifiers; a
+        // second position from here would report an extra motion event to it.
+        if (ghostty_surface_mouse_captured(_surface))
+        {
+            return;
+        }
+
+        // The button is up, and this is the first thing WT calls after a drag
+        // ends - it reports no release outside VT mouse mode - so this is where
+        // the synthesised release belongs. Without it ghostty reads these hover
+        // positions as a drag that never ended, and the selection follows the
+        // pointer with no button held.
+        _releaseMouseIfHeld();
+
+        // A move within one cell is not worth a position: ghostty re-runs link
+        // detection on every one it is given, and WT sends one per pixel.
+        const til::point cell{ terminalPosition };
+        if (_hoveredCell == cell)
+        {
+            return;
+        }
+        _hoveredCell = cell;
+        _mouseTo(terminalPosition, _keyMods);
+    }
+
+    // The pointer left the control. A negative position is what ghostty reads
+    // as "outside": it drops the highlight and reports the link gone, which is
+    // what clears _hoveredUri.
+    void GhosttyControlCore::ClearHoveredCell()
+    {
+        _hoveredCell.reset();
+
+        // Not mid-drag. A drag that leaves the control keeps the pointer
+        // captured and the selection live, and "outside" would end it.
+        if (!_surface || _closing || _mouseDown)
+        {
+            return;
+        }
+        ghostty_surface_mouse_pos(_surface, -1.0, -1.0, _keyMods);
+    }
+
+    // The URL under the pointer, or empty for none.
+    //
+    // The position is ignored, deliberately. ghostty's answer is *about* the
+    // pointer's cell and arrives as an action rather than as an answer to a
+    // question, and the one caller - ControlInteractivity's ctrl+click - asks
+    // about the cell the pointer is in. So this is the same answer, and it is
+    // the only one available: the C API has no "what is at this point" call.
+    winrt::hstring GhosttyControlCore::GetHyperlink(const Core::Point /*position*/) const { return _hoveredUri; }
+
+    winrt::hstring GhosttyControlCore::HoveredUriText() const { return _hoveredUri; }
+
+    // Where TermControl draws the tooltip. Null when no link is under the
+    // pointer, which is how it reads "nothing to show".
+    IReference<Core::Point> GhosttyControlCore::HoveredCell() const
+    {
+        return (_hoveredUri.empty() || !_hoveredCell) ? nullptr :
+                                                        IReference<Core::Point>{ _hoveredCell->to_core_point() };
+    }
+
+    void GhosttyControlCore::_selectionDroveTheMouse()
+    {
+        _hoveredCell.reset();
+        _selectionDrove = true;
+    }
 
     // Whether the application has asked for the mouse. This is the switch that
     // makes ControlInteractivity route pointer events to SendMouseEvent instead
diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.h b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.h
index e2bf66c79..c22eebb1e 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.h
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.h
@@ -269,6 +269,10 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         void _mouseTo(Core::Point cell, ghostty_input_mods_e mods) const;
         void _releaseMouseIfHeld() const;
 
+        // Said by the selection path when it has already driven the mouse for
+        // the pointer event WT is delivering. See SetHoveredCell.
+        void _selectionDroveTheMouse();
+
         // The modifiers every mouse event of a selection carries - the engine
         // reads block-vs-linear off them, per event. See GhosttySelectionMods.h.
         ghostty_input_mods_e _selectionMods() const noexcept;
@@ -333,6 +337,23 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         // _releaseMouseIfHeld.
         mutable bool _mouseDown{ false };
 
+        // The modifiers the last key event carried, which is what a hover
+        // position claims. See TrySendKeyEvent.
+        ghostty_input_mods_e _keyMods{ GHOSTTY_MODS_NONE };
+
+        // The cell the pointer was last reported at - a gate against sending a
+        // position per pixel - and the link ghostty says is under it, empty for
+        // none. The link is ghostty's answer, not a lookup: MOUSE_OVER_LINK.
+        std::optional<til::point> _hoveredCell;
+        winrt::hstring _hoveredUri;
+
+        // Whether the selection path drove the mouse for this pointer event.
+        bool _selectionDrove{ false };
+
+        // A release WT synthesised must not open a link the pointer happens to
+        // be over. See _releaseMouseIfHeld.
+        mutable bool _suppressOpenUrl{ false };
+
         // Whether the drag in progress is a block selection. Alt at the moment
         // of the click decides it, as it does on cascadia, and it holds for the
         // whole drag - releasing alt halfway through does not turn a block
